{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "כי תשה ברעך", :translit "ki tashveh bere'akha", :gloss "when you lend your neighbor"} {:he "סמך לצרעת", :translit "samakh letsara'at", :gloss "juxtaposed to tzara'at"} {:he "השאילני כלי שלך", :translit "hash'ileni kheli shelkha", :gloss "lend me your vessel"} {:he "אין לי", :translit "ein li", :gloss "I do not have"} {:he "ופנו את הבית", :translit "ufenu et habayit", :gloss "and empty the house"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "smikhut ha-parashiyot: lending laws juxtaposed to tzara'at pericope (Deut 24:8-9)"}], :frame {:text "The Torah's sequence places laws of lending (Deut 24:10) directly after the pericope of tzara'at (Deut 24:8-9). The Talmud interprets nega'im on a house as occasion for divine disclosure: when the priest orders 'Empty the house' (Leviticus 14:36), hidden wealth is revealed, exposing one who falsely denied having vessels to lend.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 24:8-10" "Leviticus 14:36" "Arakhin 16a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "לא תעשוק שכיר", :translit "lo ta'ashok sekhir", :gloss "you shall not oppress a hired worker"} {:he "סמך לכי תשה", :translit "samakh lekhi tashveh", :gloss "juxtaposed to 'when you lend'"} {:he "אעכבנו בחובי", :translit "e'akhbeno bekhovi", :gloss "I will detain him for my debt"} {:he "פרע לו שכרו מיד", :translit "pera' lo sekharo miyad", :gloss "pay him his wages immediately"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "smikhut ha-parashiyot: worker-oppresion laws juxtaposed to lending laws"}], :frame {:text "The verse prohibits oppressing a hired worker (Deuteronomy 24:14). The Talmud (Bava Metzia 112a) rules that an employer must pay wages on time (Leviticus 19:13). The juxtaposition of this law to the lending passage teaches that even when the worker owes the employer money, the employer may not withhold wages against that debt.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 24:10-14" "Leviticus 19:13" "Bava Metzia 112a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ה' א", :translit "Hashem E-lokekha", :gloss "YHWH your God"} {:he "לא תעשק", :translit "lo ta'ashok", :gloss "you shall not oppress"} {:he "מלוה ה' חונן דל", :translit "malveh Hashem khonen dal", :gloss "He who is gracious to the poor lends to YHWH"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "smikhut ha-parashiyot: divine Name juxtaposed to prohibition of oppression"}], :frame {:text "Scripture states: 'He who is gracious to the poor lends to YHWH' (Proverbs 19:17). Through this equation the Ba'al HaTurim reads the juxtaposition of the divine Name to the prohibition of oppression: harming the poor is counted as harming God Himself, since God treats kindness to the poor as a loan made directly to Him.", :sources ["Proverbs 19:17"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "smikhut ha-parashiyot: lending laws juxtaposed to tzara'at pericope (Deut 24:8-9)", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "smikhut ha-parashiyot: worker-oppresion laws juxtaposed to lending laws", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "smikhut ha-parashiyot: divine Name juxtaposed to prohibition of oppression", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:39:00.747638183Z", :model "glm-5.2"}